#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""heavy-job: a machine-wide semaphore for heavy development jobs.

Why: several Claude sessions in several projects share one machine. A project can gate its own heavy jobs
(make ci, e2e, emulators...), but nothing coordinates across projects, so emulators, e2e suites, `npm ci`,
Gradle, Playwright and full test suites pile up until the machine swaps and the kernel kills processes. This
tool gives all of them one shared pool of N slots.

How: N slot files `slot-<i>.lock` in $HEAVY_JOB_DIR (default /run/user/<uid>/heavy-job), each held with
fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB). The lock lives on the open file description, and the job inherits the fd
(`pass_fds` for `run`, an inheritable fd across exec for `hold`), so the slot stays held as long as any
process of the job's tree lives, even if the wrapper is killed with SIGKILL; the kernel releases it by itself
when the last holder dies, so there are no stale locks. While a slot is held, `slot-<i>.json` describes the
holder (pid, kind, label, cwd, project, argv, start time). A json whose slot lock is free is stale: it is
reported and ignored.

N is $HEAVY_JOB_SLOTS, else the first line of ~/.config/heavy-job/slots, else `default_slots` of the
`heavy-job` config piece (4).

Weight: a job may take several slots at once (`--weight N`, default 1; `--kind ml` defaults to 2, because a model
in RAM weighs several test runs). The N slot locks are taken all or nothing, the job gets all their fds,
HEAVY_JOB_SLOT is the comma-joined list and every slot's json carries `slots` and `weight`. A weight above the
number of slots is clamped to it with a warning.

Heavy jobs started WITHOUT the wrapper are found by `scan` (a walk over /proc of the current uid) and reduce
the number of slots a waiter may take: effective free = N - held slots - unwrapped heavy jobs. The scan skips
container processes (cgroup with `docker-` or `containerd`), processes whose environment has HEAVY_JOB_SLOT,
processes whose command line mentions `heavy-job`, and the ancestors of the process that scans; of a tree of
matching processes only the top-most counts (it is one job). Besides the classifier it also finds:
  - orphaned test workers: a vitest/jest/playwright worker whose parent is no longer its runner (`pkill vitest`
    leaves the fork-pool workers behind, parented to `systemd --user`, spinning for hours). Workers of the same
    runner and directory are one job, flagged `orphan`, with RSS and CPU share; the messages say it can be
    stopped with `kill <pid>`. Nothing is ever killed automatically;
  - resident ML models: a process above 1.5 GiB RSS that maps torch, ctranslate2, onnxruntime or tensorflow
    counts as ONE `ml` job of weight 1 (maps is read only for big processes, found by statm first).

Queue (first come, first served): a job is admitted only if it is the oldest live waiter. Without it, a
weight-2 job (`--kind ml`) starves: each time one slot frees, a later weight-1 job takes it. Design:
  - Every `run`/`hold` without `--no-wait` takes a ticket in $HEAVY_JOB_DIR/queue/ the moment it arrives, named
    `<time_ns, 20 digits>-<pid>.ticket` (json: pid, weight, kind, label, project, argv...). The waiter keeps an
    flock on its ticket for as long as it lives. Same trick as the slots: the kernel drops the lock when the
    process dies, even by SIGKILL, so a ticket whose lock is free is dead and is deleted by whoever sees it (no
    pid reuse problem, nothing to clean by hand). The ticket is created under a hidden name, locked and only then
    renamed into place, so nobody ever sees a live ticket without its lock.
  - Waiters look at the live tickets in order. Only the oldest one probes the slots (and the scan, and the
    admission rules above); the others wait for their turn, even if a slot is free and their weight would fit.
    That head-of-line blocking is the point: a big job cannot be overtaken.
  - The head takes its slots, stamps them (`claim_slots`) and only then drops its ticket, so the next head
    already sees them held.
  - `--no-wait` takes no ticket: it exits 75 if any live waiter exists (all of them arrived earlier), else it
    behaves as before. `--max-wait S` (default: no limit) or Ctrl-C drop the ticket too.
  - `status` lists the queue in order (`queue` in --json) and a job that arrives now is not admitted while it is
    not empty. Running jobs and `hold` keep their slots exactly as before; waiters of the previous version have
    no ticket and keep polling the slots as they did (they can still overtake until they end).

Pools: compiling apps and running tests saturate the CPU; installing dependencies, type checks and linters
do not, and must not wait behind them for an hour. So there are two pools, each with its own slots and its own queue:
  - cpu: the N slots above, files `slot-<i>.lock` and `queue/` exactly as before, so waiters of the previous
    version keep working. Tests, e2e, builds, gates, emulators, ML, and anything not proven light.
  - light: $HEAVY_JOB_LIGHT_SLOTS, else ~/.config/heavy-job/light-slots, else 3 slots (0 turns the pool off), in the
    subdirectory `light/` with the same file names; shown as `hueco <N+j> (resto)`. Its jobs get
    HEAVY_JOB_LIGHT_SLOT (not HEAVY_JOB_SLOT, which projects read as "a heavy slot is held", so a suite started
    inside a light job still queues for a cpu slot) and HEAVY_JOB_POOL.
  The command decides the pool (`pool_for`), not the declared kind, because `--kind gate` labels both `make ci`
  and `npx tsc -b`: a command with anything heavy other than an install goes to cpu whatever --kind says; one made
  only of installs, known light tools (tsc, eslint, ruff, mypy, `npm run lint`, `make typecheck`...) and trivial
  commands (cd, echo) goes to light; an opaque one (a script) goes to light only with a light --kind (install,
  typecheck, lint, light). A light job's admission counts the holders of both pools (memory is one for the
  machine); a cpu job's counts only the cpu pool, so a running `tsc` never turns on the memory rule or the settle
  for the cpu queue (`admission_inputs`). The light pool has its own threshold ($HEAVY_JOB_LIGHT_MIN_AVAIL_GIB or ~/.config/heavy-job/light-min-available-gib,
  default 1.5 GiB: a large `tsc` peaks well below it) and settle ($HEAVY_JOB_LIGHT_SETTLE, 20 s).

Admission: once the slots are free, two more rules apply, but only while another heavy job is running (a slot
held by someone else, or an unwrapped job); with nothing else running a low MemAvailable is not the job's
fault, so it starts anyway, with a warning, instead of starving:
  - memory: MemAvailable >= threshold x weight. The threshold is $HEAVY_JOB_MIN_AVAIL_GIB, else the first line
    of ~/.config/heavy-job/min-available-gib, else 2.5 GiB; 0 disables the rule;
  - settle: the previous holder must have taken its slots at least $HEAVY_JOB_SETTLE seconds ago (default 45),
    because memory does not reflect a job that has just started (two jobs launched within 2 s would both see
    the same free memory). The start is the mtime of the lock file, stamped when the slots are taken.
The waiting message and the exit-75 message say which rule holds the job back.

The classifier decides which command lines are heavy. It is pure and lives in lib/heavy_job_classifier.py,
which the PreToolUse gate imports by itself:

    classify_command('cd x && ./gradlew assembleDebug')   # ('build', '...') or None
    classify_argv(['npm', 'ci'])                          # ('install', '...') or None

  classify_command sees through `heavy-job run|hold -- CMD`: it judges the inner CMD.
  classify_outside_semaphore(command) is the hook's entry point: same, but a real `heavy-job run|hold -- CMD`
  is covered and not judged, while every other simple command of the line still is.

Kinds: emulator, build, install, e2e, tests, gate, render (a video encode: `ffmpeg` writing video, `make video*`,
`remotion render`; not a single frame nor audio only), ml (whisper, faster-whisper, whisperx, whisper-ctranslate2,
torchrun, `accelerate launch`, `python -m whisper|whisperx|faster_whisper|torch.distributed.run`). Only the program that is really executed counts; text that
is an argument or a quoted string of another program is never flagged.

Usage:
  heavy-job run  [--kind K] [--label L] [--weight N] [--max-wait S] [--no-wait] -- CMD...
      Wait for N slots (and for the admission rules), run CMD as a child with HEAVY_JOB_SLOT=<i[,j...]> and
      HEAVY_JOB_LABEL in its environment, forward SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP, exit with the child's code (128+n if
      killed by signal n).
  heavy-job hold [same options] -- CMD...
      For long-lived daemons (the Android emulator): take a slot and exec CMD in place, so the slot is held
      exactly as long as that process (and the children that inherit the fd) lives.
  heavy-job status [--json]        slots (a multi-slot job once), unwrapped heavy jobs, MemAvailable, swap, load,
                                   admission thresholds and whether a weight-1 job would start now (and why not)
  heavy-job scan [--json]          heavy processes NOT under the semaphore
  heavy-job classify [--json] [--stdin] [-- COMMAND_STRING]
      Is this shell command line heavy? exit 0 heavy, 1 light.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 2 usage error, 75 no slot or older waiters (--no-wait, or --max-wait S elapsed; by default it waits without limit), 126/127 the
command could not be executed, 130 interrupted while waiting.

Environment (besides the above): HEAVY_JOB_POLL seconds between checks while waiting (default 5);
HEAVY_JOB_SCAN=0 disables the scan in the slot calculation (tests, escape hatch); HEAVY_JOB_MIN_AVAIL_GIB and
HEAVY_JOB_SETTLE as above; HEAVY_JOB_MEMINFO overrides /proc/meminfo and HEAVY_JOB_ML_MIN_RSS_MIB the 1536 MiB
threshold of the ML scan (both for tests).
"""
